	<item>
		<title><![CDATA[What Happens to Muscle During and After Limb Lengthening?]]></title>
		<link>https://premierlimblengthening.com/what-happens-to-muscle-during-and-after-limb-lengthening/</link>
		<pubDate>Fri, 12 Dec 2025 04:36:00 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[pll-edusenbury]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://premierlimblengthening.com/?p=101</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Our bones and muscles function as an integrated system. Each relies on the other for strength, stability, and movement, and when one changes, the other must adapt. It’s a relationship that’s important to consider during limb lengthening – the goal is to safely increase bone length while supporting the surrounding soft tissues as they respond to that change.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Limb lengthening entails controlled bone division and the use of mechanical devices to gradually separate the bone, allowing new bone to form in the gap; this is called distraction osteogenesis. Focus is primarily on the bone itself, but this controlled lengthening also places continuous demands on the muscles, tendons, nerves, and blood vessels that surround it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"anchor":"the-anatomy"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="the-anatomy">The Anatomy</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Limb lengthening can include the arms, but most commonly involves the lower extremities, particularly the femur (thigh bone) and tibia (shin bone). These are the long bones of the body, and they’re surrounded by large, powerful muscle groups responsible for walking, balance, and posture.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In femoral lengthening, the primary muscle groups involved include the quadriceps at the front of the thigh, the hamstrings at the back, and the adductor muscles along the inner thigh. Tibial lengthening primarily affects the gastrocnemius and soleus muscles of the calf, as well as the tibialis anterior along the front of the lower leg. Our muscles are connected to bone through tendons and are encased in layers of fascia, all of which must adapt as the limb gradually lengthens.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>During surgery, the bone is carefully divided and stabilized using an <a href="/fixation-methods-in-limb-lengthening-internal-vs-external/">internal</a> lengthening device. Changes to muscle tension are immediate, and can result in protective tightening, swelling, and temporary weakness – an expected reaction to surgical trauma and altered biomechanics.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>As distraction begins, the muscles and tendons are under a sustained stretch. Despite their adaptive capacity, these soft tissues are subject to mechanical stress that can be uncomfortable. Muscle fibers respond to lengthening by adding new sarcomeres, the contractile units that muscles are made of. In other words, as the bone lengthens, the resulting mechanical demand on the muscle triggers structural changes at the cellular level to accommodate increased bone length while maintaining functional strength.1</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Once the desired length has been achieved, the consolidation phase begins: newly formed bone hardens, and muscles continue to remodel and recover. Muscle mass, fiber size, and functional strength gradually improve as normal loading and activity levels increase, particularly when rehabilitation is consistent and appropriately guided. However, it can still take up to two years to achieve preoperative performance levels, depending on the extent of lengthening.2</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"anchor":"surgical-accommodations"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="surgical-accommodations">Surgical Accommodations</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Because soft tissues do not always adapt at the same pace as bone, limb lengthening could require proactive strategies to prevent complications. Surgeons try to anticipate areas where excess tension may develop and plan interventions that support the muscles, tendons, and nerves throughout the lengthening process.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>In some cases, additional surgical procedures are used to reduce soft-tissue tension by selectively releasing tight muscles, tendons, fascia, or nerves that cannot safely accommodate the increasing bone length on their own.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The need for a release surgery and whether it’s performed at the time of the initial surgery or later depend on the bone being lengthened, the planned lengthening, and the patient’s baseline flexibility. For example, during tibial lengthening, the Achilles tendon is particularly prone to tightening. If left unaddressed, this can limit ankle motion or lead to fixed contractures (a condition in which a joint becomes stiff and permanently restricted in its range of motion). In such cases, a controlled surgical reorientation of the Achilles tendon via Z-plasty or tenotomy allows the ankle to maintain mobility as the tibia lengthens. Unrelieved tension in the soft tissues can contribute to muscle degeneration, fibrosis (the buildup of stiff connective tissue), and joint stiffness, regardless of the surgical site.3</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Other necessary accommodating procedures could involve muscle fascia release or nerve decompression when persistent or progressive tightness, joint immobility, <a href="/limb-lengthening-pain-the-truth/">pain</a>, nerve irritation, or related symptoms develop. Releasing tight fascia helps maintain muscle excursion (the distance it can release and contract), and addressing compressed nerves prevents neuropraxia (a nerve signal “traffic jam” without physical damage) or permanent nerve injury, allowing the bone lengthening process to continue.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"anchor":"non-surgical-support"} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="non-surgical-support">Non-Surgical Support</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="/the-importance-of-physical-therapy-in-limb-lengthening/">Physical therapy (PT)</a> is one of the most important non-surgical tools for supporting muscle adaptation. As the bone gradually lengthens, targeted therapy maintains joint range of motion, reduces muscle stiffness, and reinforces proper movement patterns at appropriate intervals of recovery.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>PT participation usually takes place in the office multiple times per week during the distraction phase, with additional stretching and strengthening exercises performed at home. Therapy focuses on preserving flexibility, preventing contractures, and maintaining strength despite temporary mobility limitations. If muscle tightness progresses or functional motion declines, surgeons may adjust lengthening to allow soft tissues to recover.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Physical therapy continues into the consolidation phase, rebuilding muscle mass, restoring endurance, and progressively strengthening both muscle and bone through graded resistance exercises, weight-bearing as permitted, proprioceptive training (nervous system awareness of limb position and coordinated joint movement), and functional movement retraining. This phase is critical for reversing muscle atrophy, improving coordination, and supporting a safe return to daily activities.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Other non-surgical support strategies help muscles adapt safely throughout limb lengthening, including:</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list -->
<ul class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Stretching and Positioning – Daily, structured stretching guided by your care team helps counteract prolonged muscle tension. Proper positioning during rest and sleep can also reduce the risk of joint stiffness and contracture development.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Pain Management – Muscle soreness and spasms are common during distraction. Medications, application of hot or cold packs, and adjunctive therapies can mitigate discomfort and help patients stay engaged in PT.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Nutrition and Hydration – Your body is working overtime to heal itself, and poor nutritional status can slow recovery; your care team will help you manage nutritional guidelines.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Activity Modulation – Balancing movement with rest is essential; surgeons and therapists will help guide safe activity levels throughout each phase.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Close Monitoring and Communication – Regular follow-up appointments and imaging allow the care team to adjust the lengthening plan accordingly.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ul>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Limb lengthening requires both the patient and the surgeon to respect the limits of muscle adaptation and the individual’s overall capacity to physiologically adjust to the procedure. If you have questions about limb lengthening or associated concerns, <a href="/consult/">get in touch</a> with the team at <a href="/">Premier Limb Lengthening Institute</a>.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:list {"ordered":true} -->
<ol class="wp-block-list">
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Lindsey, C. A., Makarov, M. R., Shoemaker, S., Birch, J. G., Buschang , P. H., Cherkashin, A. M., Welch, R. D., &amp; Samchukov, M. L. (2000). The Effect of the Amount of Limb Lengthening on Skeletal Muscle. Clinical Orthopaedics &amp; Related Research, 402, 278–287. Ovid.com. <a href="https://www.ovid.com/jnls/clinorthop/abstract/00003086-200209000-00028" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.ovid.com/jnls/clinorthop/abstract/00003086-200209000-00028</a>.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Barker, K. L., Lamb, S. E., &amp; Simpson, H. R. (2010). Recovery of muscle strength and power after limb-lengthening surgery. Archives of physical medicine and rehabilitation, 91(3), 384–388. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apmr.2009.11.014" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apmr.2009.11.014</a>.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
<!-- wp:list-item -->
<li>Cai, G., Coulton, L., Yang, L., &amp; Saleh, M. (2003). THE STRUCTURAL RESPONSE OF MUSCLE TO LIMB LENGTHENING. Orthopaedic Proceedings, 85-B(SUPP_I), 12-12. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1302/0301-620X.85BSUPP\_I.0850012c" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://doi.org/10.1302/0301-620X.85BSUPP\_I.0850012c</a>.</li>
<!-- /wp:list-item -->
</ol>
<!-- /wp:list -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p><a href="/category/after-limb-lengthening/">After Limb Lengthening</a> <a href="/category/bone-health/">Bone Health</a> <a href="/category/impact-on-the-body/">Impact On The Body</a> <a href="/category/limb-lengthening/">Limb Lengthening</a> <a href="/category/paying-for-limb-lengthening/">Paying For Limb Lengthening</a></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
